    "postFreezeLabelCorrections": [
      {
        "id": "000039-005",
        "check": "wordiness-and-redundancy/wordiness",
        "before": true,
        "after": false,
        "reason": "The complete text makes daytime self-consumption relevant to savings. 'The extent to which' expresses degree, while 'you are able to' preserves capacity rather than actual consumption; 'as it is produced' supplies the timing. 'The amount you save depends on how much of the electricity you can use as it is produced' preserves the homeowner, degree, ability, and timing. That alternative establishes a shorter paraphrase, but the original uses ordinary formal expressions for those distinctions. Identifiable padding beyond that paraphrase has not been established."
      },
      {
        "id": "000039-103",
        "check": "wordiness-and-redundancy/wordiness",
        "before": true,
        "after": false,
        "reason": "The writer requested evidence about a permanent maker's mark; one supplier supplied a photograph and the other was still asking its mill. 'Provide an indication of whether' permits tentative evidence, whereas 'confirm whether' would strengthen the requested answer. 'I asked each supplier to indicate whether the glass carries a permanent maker's mark' preserves the suppliers, past request, scope, and tentative evidential force. It is shorter, but the original is ordinary useful formal wording. The separate 'in advance before the delivery' repeats payment timing and belongs to redundancy; 'make a payment' is an ordinary transactional expression. The complete text supplies no independently established wordiness hit."
      },
      {
        "id": "000039-149",
        "check": "wordiness-and-redundancy/wordiness",
        "before": true,
        "after": false,
        "reason": "The instruction asks the packer to evaluate the corners before fitting protection; the following sentences explain how fragile mouldings affect that judgment. 'Assess the corners before fitting protective pieces over them' preserves the implied actor, evaluative action, object, and timing. 'Make an assessment' is an ordinary formal expression for that useful action, analogous to an inspection instruction. Its nominalized form and the available shorter verb alone establish no unnecessary elaboration under the frozen definition. Ordinary formal wording needs no separately named technical procedure to qualify for protection."
      }
    ]
  },
